    # ------------------------------------------------------------ parallel patterns
    Q('reduction', 'Parallel patterns', 'm',
      'Write a shared memory block reduction and explain why sequential addressing beats interleaved addressing.',
      'Which threads are active in each step?',
      'Each block loads its values into shared memory, then for stride = blockDim.x / 2 down to 1 the first stride threads add s[t + stride] into s[t], with a barrier after each step. Thread 0 then holds the block sum. With sequential addressing the active threads are packed into the first warps, so whole warps idle instead of diverging, and the accesses hit different banks. Interleaved addressing (t % (2 × stride) == 0) spreads active threads over every warp.',
      '<p>For 512 threads: 9 levels, 511 additions, 10 barriers including the one after loading. The last 5 levels can use warp shuffles instead of shared memory.</p>',
      kind='Coding', lang='cuda',
      code='__global__ void blockSum(const float* x, float* out, int n) {\n    extern __shared__ float s[];\n    int t = threadIdx.x, i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + t;\n    s[t] = i < n ? x[i] : 0.0f;\n    __syncthreads();\n    for (int stride = blockDim.x / 2; stride > 0; stride /= 2) {\n        if (t < stride) s[t] += s[t + stride];\n        __syncthreads();\n    }\n    if (t == 0) atomicAdd(out, s[0]);\n}',
      points=['Barrier after every level', 'Active threads packed', 'One atomic per block']),
